{"type":"text","timestamp":1782090828863,"sessionID":"ses_11326b3aaffebuPmo4MbnyTU6H","part":{"id":"prt_eece3f5370017etaIAXmdbnRD0","messageID":"msg_eece3d925001h1KzQxcyixsJkk","sessionID":"ses_11326b3aaffebuPmo4MbnyTU6H","type":"text","text":"Done. Implemented a complete Brazilian Soccer MCP server per `TASK.md`.\n\n**What was built** (all in the current directory):\n- `pyproject.toml` — build/install config with `brazilian-soccer-mcp` console script.\n- `brazilian_soccer_mcp/` — `normalizers.py` (team-key/date/competition normalization), `data_loader.py` (loads all 6 CSVs into unified `Match` records, dedupes cross-file overlap → 22,733 unique matches + 18,207 players), `queries.py` (`QueryEngine` with matches/H2H/team-stats/player-search/standings/champion/relegation/avg-goals/biggest-wins/best-record), `server.py` (FastMCP server, 16 tools).\n- `tests/` — BDD GWT feature files (`match_queries`, `player_queries`, `competition_queries`) + step defs, plus `test_normalizers.py` unit tests. Each code file opens with a context-block comment.\n- `README.md` updated.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `pip install -e .` succeeds.\n- `pytest -q` → **33 passed**.\n- MCP tool invocation verified end-to-end (`call_tool('find_matches', ...)` returns match JSON).\n- Spot checks: `champion(\"Brasileirao\", 2019)` → Flamengo; `top_brazilian_players(3)` → Neymar Jr, Coutinho, Marcelo.\n\nRun the server with `brazilian-soccer-mcp` (stdio) or `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp.server`.","time":{"start":1782090822967,"end":1782090828860}}}
